<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">De-Stalinization</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as De-Stalinisation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">De-Stalinization was a series of political reforms in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> on 5 March 1953. It began quietly, with the release of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> prisoners and unpublicised rehabilitations, and became official on 25 February 1956, when Khrushchev's speech to a closed session of the 20th Party Congress laid out some of Stalin's crimes and denounced his cult of personality. Monuments to Stalin were removed, his name was taken off places and out of the state anthem, and on 31 October 1961 his body was moved from the Lenin Mausoleum. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and Albania, which condemned Khrushchev as a revisionist, cited it as a major cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Stalin's death, <b>de-Stalinization</b> made the lines about him unacceptable, and from 1956 to 1977 the anthem was played without words.</blockquote><a href="/songs/state-anthem-of-the-soviet-union">State Anthem of the Soviet Union</a></li><li><blockquote>During <b>de-Stalinization</b> the stanza naming Stalin was dropped from public performance and replaced with a new one about comradeship and proletarian internationalism.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wide-is-my-motherland">Wide Is My Motherland</a></li></ul></section>
